    },
    {
      "title": "What will you learn?",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "By the end of this chapter, you should understand how to:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Create and manage Logical Volume Manager (LVM) storage."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Create logical volumes for files and swap space."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Add more storage to an existing volume group."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Increase a logical volume's size without deleting its existing files."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Remove a disk from a volume group safely."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Verify storage using Linux commands."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The main idea is simple: LVM makes disk space easier to manage and expand."
        }
      ],
      "words": 72
    },
    {
      "title": "1. Why do we need LVM?",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "In the previous chapter, you learned how to create partitions and file systems."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Imagine you have a server with a 100 GB disk. You create a 20 GB partition for application data."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "After some months, the application needs 35 GB."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "With basic partitions, increasing the space can require additional partition and file-system management. With LVM, if the volume group has enough free space, you can extend the logical volume and its file system more easily."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "henzelmoras.github.io"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "LVM adds a flexible storage-management layer between physical storage and the file systems you use."
        }
      ],
      "words": 91
    },
    {
      "title": "2. Understand the four main LVM concepts",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Think of LVM as a storage warehouse."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "1. Physical device"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The actual storage device, such as a disk or partition."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Example: /dev/vdb1"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "2. Physical Volume (PV)"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "A disk or partition initialized for use by LVM."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Command: pvcreate"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "3. Volume Group (VG)"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "A pool of storage made from one or more physical volumes."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Command: vgcreate"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "4. Logical Volume (LV)"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "A storage area allocated from the volume group's available space. You can put a file system or swap space on it."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Command: lvcreate"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Remember the order"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Physical device → PV → VG → LV → File system → Mount point"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "For example:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/dev/vdb1 is your partition."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "pvcreate prepares it for LVM."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "vgcreate combines it into a storage pool."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lvcreate allocates part of that pool."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "mkfs.xfs creates a file system."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "mount makes the files accessible."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Do not confuse a PV with a VG or an LV. Each serves a different purpose."
        }
      ],
      "words": 148
    },
    {
      "title": "3. What are physical extents and logical extents?",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "LVM divides storage into small units called extents."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Physical extent (PE): A small unit of space on a physical volume."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Logical extent (LE): A small unit of space belonging to a logical volume."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "LVM maps logical extents to physical extents. You usually do not need to manage each extent individually, but understanding them helps when reading commands such as lvdisplay."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "For example, if an extent is 4 MiB, then 100 extents represent 400 MiB of storage."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The actual extent size depends on the volume group configuration."
        }
      ],
      "words": 86
    },
    {
      "title": "4. Create a logical volume step by step",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "We will use an example based on the RH134 guided exercise. The example assumes a disposable test disk with two suitable partitions, /dev/vdb1 and /dev/vdb2."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Do not run these commands on your system disk or on partitions containing data you need."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 1: Check available storage"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lsblk"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo pvs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo vgs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo lvs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "These commands show disks and the current LVM configuration."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "pvs — physical volumes."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "vgs — volume groups."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lvs — logical volumes."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "If the last three commands show no LVM storage, that may simply mean you have not configured any yet."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 2: Prepare physical volumes"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo pvcreate /dev/vdb1 /dev/vdb2"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This initializes both partitions for LVM."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Expected message:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Physical volume \"/dev/vdb1\" successfully created"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The exact output may vary."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 3: Create a volume group"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo vgcreate data_vg /dev/vdb1 /dev/vdb2"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This creates a storage pool called data_vg using both physical volumes."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Verify:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo vgs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "You should see data_vg in the output."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 4: Create a logical volume"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo lvcreate -n data_lv -L 400M data_vg"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Understand the command:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lvcreate — create a logical volume."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "-n data_lv — name the logical volume data_lv."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "-L 400M — allocate 400 MiB."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "data_vg — use the storage pool named data_vg."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The logical volume will normally be accessible as:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/dev/data_vg/data_lv"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Verify:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo lvs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 5: Create a file system"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo mkfs.xfs /dev/data_vg/data_lv"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This creates an XFS file system on the logical volume."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Remember: creating the logical volume does not automatically create a file system."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 6: Create a mount point"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo mkdir /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This creates the directory through which you will access the logical volume."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 7: Mount the logical volume"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo mount /dev/data_vg/data_lv /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Verify:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "findmnt /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "df -h /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Your logical volume is now available through /data."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 8: Make the mount persistent"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "First, find the file system UUID:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo blkid /dev/data_vg/data_lv"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Edit the configuration file:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo vim /etc/fstab"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Add a line using your actual UUID:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "UUID=your-actual-uuid  /data  xfs  defaults  0  0"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Then test the configuration:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo findmnt --verify --verbose"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo mount -a"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "findmnt /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The mount should remain configured after reboot."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Practical lesson: LVM manages the storage allocation. XFS manages the files. /etc/fstab configures persistent mounting."
        }
      ],
      "words": 354
    },
    {
      "title": "5. Display LVM information",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "You must be able to inspect your storage before changing it."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Command"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "What it shows"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "pvs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Physical volumes and their space"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "pvdisplay"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Detailed physical-volume information"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "vgs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Volume groups and free space"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "vgdisplay data_vg"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Details about one volume group"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lvs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Logical volumes and their sizes"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lvdisplay /dev/data_vg/data_lv"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Details about one logical volume"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lsblk"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Disks, partitions, and storage relationships"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "df -h /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Space available in the mounted file system"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "For example:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo vgdisplay data_vg"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Look for the free space. This tells you how much additional storage may be available for logical volumes."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Important: The size of the volume group is not necessarily the same as the size of the logical volume. A volume group can contain unused space that you allocate later."
        }
      ],
      "words": 120
    },
    {
      "title": "6. Extend a logical volume",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This is one of the most important skills in this chapter."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Suppose /data is on a 400 MiB logical volume. Later, you need 300 MiB more space."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "If the volume group has enough free space, you can increase the logical volume without recreating it."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 1: Check free space"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo vgs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo lvs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Confirm that the volume group has enough free space for the increase."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 2: Extend the logical volume"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo lvextend -L +300M /dev/data_vg/data_lv"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The + sign matters."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "-L +300M means add 300 MiB to the current size."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "-L 700M means set the logical volume's final size to 700 MiB."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Do not confuse these two forms."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 3: Extend the XFS file system"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Increasing the logical volume does not always increase the file system automatically."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Run:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo xfs_growfs /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The XFS file system must be mounted for this command."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "An alternative, where supported, is to extend the volume and resize the file system in one operation:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo lvextend -r -L +300M /dev/data_vg/data_lv"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Use one approach or the other, not both for the same intended increase."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 4: Verify the new size"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "df -h /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo lvs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The file system should now report the increased capacity."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "What if the file system is ext4?"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "For an ext4 file system, use resize2fs instead of xfs_growfs:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo lvextend -L +300M /dev/data_vg/data_lv"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo resize2fs /dev/data_vg/data_lv"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The correct resize command depends on the file system type."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "File system"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Resize command"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "XFS"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "xfs_growfs /mountpoint"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "ext4"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "resize2fs /dev/vg_name/lv_name"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Remember: Extending the LV gives the file system more underlying storage. Resizing the file system lets it use that extra storage."
        }
      ],
      "words": 263
    },
    {
      "title": "7. Extend a volume group with another disk",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "What happens when the volume group has no free space?"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "You can add another suitable disk or partition to the volume group."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Suppose /dev/vdb3 is a new, unused partition."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 1: Prepare it as a physical volume"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo pvcreate /dev/vdb3"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 2: Add it to the volume group"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo vgextend data_vg /dev/vdb3"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This adds the physical volume's available storage to data_vg."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 3: Verify"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo pvs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo vgs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The volume group should now have more capacity available."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 4: Allocate the new space to a logical volume"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "For example:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo lvextend -L +1G /dev/data_vg/data_lv"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "If the file system is XFS:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo xfs_growfs /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Verify:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "df -h /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This illustrates why LVM is useful: you can add physical storage to a pool and then allocate it to a logical volume when needed."
        }
      ],
      "words": 131
    },
    {
      "title": "8. Remove a physical volume from a volume group",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Sometimes a disk needs replacement or removal."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Do not remove a physical volume while it still contains extents used by logical volumes. First, migrate its allocated data to other physical volumes in the same volume group."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Suppose /dev/vdb3 is the physical volume you want to remove from data_vg."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 1: Check the volume group"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo pvs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo vgs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo lvs -a -o +devices"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Confirm which physical volumes contain the logical volumes' data and whether other volumes have enough free space."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 2: Move allocated data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo pvmove /dev/vdb3"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This moves allocated extents from /dev/vdb3 to other suitable physical volumes in the same volume group."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "It can fail if the remaining physical volumes do not have enough space."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 3: Remove the physical volume from the group"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo vgreduce data_vg /dev/vdb3"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This removes the now-unused physical volume from data_vg."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Step 4: Verify"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo pvs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo vgs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The physical volume should no longer belong to data_vg."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "pvmove moves allocated data."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "vgreduce removes a physical volume from a volume group."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "These are different operations. Removing a PV from a VG does not mean you should immediately format or disconnect the disk without checking its status."
        }
      ],
      "words": 192
    },
    {
      "title": "9. Managing logical volumes used as swap",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "A logical volume can hold swap space instead of a normal file system."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "For example, a swap LV could be initialized and activated like this:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo mkswap /dev/data_vg/swap_lv"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo swapon /dev/data_vg/swap_lv"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "To extend swap space, the process differs from extending XFS or ext4:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Check the volume group's free space."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Deactivate the swap LV with swapoff."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Extend the LV with lvextend."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Initialize the expanded LV again with mkswap."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Reactivate it with swapon."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Check its persistent configuration."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Example commands:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo vgs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo swapoff /dev/data_vg/swap_lv"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo lvextend -L +512M /dev/data_vg/swap_lv"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo mkswap /dev/data_vg/swap_lv"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo swapon /dev/data_vg/swap_lv"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo swapon --show"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "These commands assume that the named LV already exists and is configured as swap. The system must have enough available memory to deactivate the swap safely."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "If the swap LV is listed in /etc/fstab by UUID, reinitializing it with mkswap may change that UUID. Update the entry if necessary before relying on it after reboot."
        }
      ],
      "words": 151
