    {
      "title": "10. Common mistakes and troubleshooting",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Mistake 1: The volume group has no free space"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Check:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo vgs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "If there is no free space, you may need to add another physical volume with pvcreate and vgextend."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Mistake 2: The logical volume grew, but df -h shows the old size"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "You may have extended the logical volume without resizing the file system."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "For XFS:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo xfs_growfs /data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "For ext4:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo resize2fs /dev/data_vg/data_lv"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Mistake 3: You are unsure which physical volume contains data"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Check:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo lvs -a -o +devices"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "This helps you identify which physical devices hold logical-volume data before you attempt to remove a physical volume."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Mistake 4: You cannot create a physical volume"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Check the device first:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "lsblk"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo pvs"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "sudo wipefs -n /dev/vdb3"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The last command inspects known file-system signatures without erasing them. Ensure the device is the correct one and that you understand any existing data or signatures before proceeding."
        }
      ],
      "words": 146
